.TH "GETLTSCFG" "1" "20060929" "" ""
.SH "NAME"
getltscfg \- get LTSP configuration variables
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fIgetltscfg\fR [options] [keyword]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
getltscfg gets the configuration values defined in /etc/lts.conf, and outputs those values in in a way meant to be evaluated in a shell script.

.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP 3n
\-a, \-\-all
output all keywords
.TP 3n
\-c, \-\-configfile
use an alternate configuration file. default is
\fI/etc/lts.conf\fR
.TP 3n
\-h, \-\-help
displays help
.TP 3n
\-n, \-\-hostname
look for configurations specific to the given hostname
.TP 3n
\-v, \-\-verbose
increase the verbosity
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP 3n
0
the file was read completely. A missing closing quote is repaired \(em the
value runs to the end of the line \(em and reported on stderr, but is not an
error.
.TP 3n
2
the file could not be opened.
.TP 3n
3
a syntax error. Everything before it is printed as usual; everything after
it is missing. The line is named on stderr.
.SH "NOTES"
With \fB\-a\fR the output is one shell assignment per line, followed by the
matching \fBexport\fR lines, meant for a shell to evaluate. Values are printed
in double quotes as they are, so a value that itself holds an unmatched quote
does not parse; ltsp_config.d/01-getltscfg checks each line before evaluating
it and leaves such a line out. Do not \fBeval\fR the whole output at once in a
script that must not die: a syntax error inside \fBeval\fR ends a
non\-interactive shell outright, whatever follows it.
